<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; margin-top: 5px;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Panama's Wildlife</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Daily Habits of Animals</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Explain how to play a common game. Interactive: Exchange simple ideas about current events in group discussions.</p>
      <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can explain the rules of a typical game related to wildlife or nature themes (e.g., "I will describe the animal. You guess.").
Can describe and ask questions about current changes in animal behavior in a group discussion (e.g., "Jaguars now hunt closer to rivers because the prey moves there to find water.").
Can exchange ideas about current news related to wildlife or habitats in a group discussion (e.g., "There is news about volunteers protecting sea turtle nests in Veraguas.").</p>
    </td>
  </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
      <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>
        (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows pictures of 2-3 well-known Panamanian animals (e.g., sloth, jaguar, harpy eagle). Students play a quick "Guess the Animal" game where the teacher describes a daily habit or habitat, and students guess. For example, "This animal lives in the forest. It moves very slowly. What is it?" (Sloth). This activates prior vocabulary like 'forest', 'live', 'move', and 'slowly'.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to describe animals using present simple and simple comparatives. "The harpy eagle hunts quickly. It is stronger than the hawk." The teacher models pronunciation of long and short vowel sounds in key vocabulary using phonetic symbols and clear repetition: (/sneɪk/) "snake", (/iːg/) "eagle", (/frɒg/) "frog", (/bæt/) "bat". Also, models syllable division in "wild-life".</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms and concepts.<br>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is a habitat? Is it a place where an animal lives or a type of food?" (It's a place where an animal lives.)</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If an animal is 'nocturnal', does it sleep during the day or the night?" (It sleeps during the day.)</li>
        </ul></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___<br>5-7 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>
        (Input of new language/content)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short audio clip (or reads aloud) about recent news concerning a Panamanian animal's daily habits, e.g., "News Update: Sea Turtles in Veraguas" or "Jaguars Changing Hunting Habits." The input will use present simple for habits, 'there is/are' for description, and some comparative/superlative adjectives.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Speaking: Interactive: Exchange simple ideas about current events in group discussions.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen/read for gist and specific information to answer questions.<br>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What animal is the news about?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The jaguars now hunt closer to rivers because there is more prey there." (True) "Volunteers are protecting sea turtle nests in Bocas del Toro." (False - Veraguas)</li>
        </ul></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___<br>8-10 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>
        (Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking fluency and accuracy using comparative and superlative adjectives, "There is/there are," and present simple to describe daily habits. Reinforce vocabulary (habitat, wildlife, jaguar, forest, predator, prey, species, adaptation, protect, live, hunt, survive, endangered, nocturnal, wild, diverse). Practice pronunciation of long/short vowels and syllable division.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b><br>
        <ol>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice: "Complete the Sentence" (Oral):</b> Teacher displays sentences with gaps related to animal habits and habitats. Students orally complete them. E.g., "The sloth _____ (move) slowly." "There ____ (be) many ______ (endangered/wild) species in Panama." "The harpy eagle is ______ (strong) than the hawk." Focus on correct verb forms and adjective use.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice: "Sentence Chain with Comparatives":</b> Students sit in a circle. The first student says, "The [Animal A] is [Comparative Adjective] than the [Animal B]." The next student takes [Animal B] and makes a new comparative sentence. E.g., S1: "The eagle is larger than the parrot." S2: "The parrot is smaller than the toucan." Ensure varied vocabulary use.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction: "Describe and Guess":</b> In pairs, one student describes an animal's habits or habitat without naming it, using learned grammar and vocabulary. The other student guesses. E.g., S1: "This animal is nocturnal. It lives in the forest. It hunts prey. It is a predator." S2: "Is it a jaguar?" S1: "Yes, it is." Encourage use of "I will describe the animal. You guess." and 'there is/are'.</li>
        </ol></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___<br>15-18 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>
        (Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Panama's Wildlife News Discussion":</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a card with a short, simple "news headline" about a change in a Panamanian animal's behavior or habitat (e.g., "Sea turtle nests are safer this year because volunteers protect them," or "Jaguars are now hunting closer to rivers to find water," or "The forest is hotter now, so some species migrate faster.").</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students discuss the news within their groups. They describe the animal's usual habits, comment on the change, explain possible reasons using comparative adjectives (e.g., "The river is wetter than the forest," "This year is hotter than last year"), and ask questions to each other. They must use "there is/are" and present simple to describe habits and current situations. The teacher circulates, facilitating discussion and noting language use.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Finally, each group briefly reports one interesting point from their discussion to the class, exchanging ideas and demonstrating collaborative interaction about an environmental topic.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___<br>10-12 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>
        (Formative assessment strategy)</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on speaking output during the "Panama's Wildlife News Discussion" and group report.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate actively in the group discussion and contribute to the brief class report, demonstrating their ability to describe animal habits, use comparatives, and discuss current events related to wildlife.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b><br>
        <ul>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (long/short vowels like "snake," "frog") and clear syllable division in compound nouns like "wild-life."</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of comparative adjectives (e.g., "stronger than," "faster"), "there is/there are," and present simple for habits.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in describing animal habits, habitats, and changes in behavior.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Active and respectful collaboration within the group discussion, facilitating turn-taking.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Integration of at least 5 target vocabulary words (e.g., jaguar, forest, prey, protect, endangered, adapt).</li>
        </ul></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b><br>
        <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words or phrases did I use today to talk about Panamanian animals and their habits?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy or difficult for me when discussing animal news with my group?"</li>
        </ul></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___<br>7-8 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b><br>
        <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well did I explain my ideas about the animals' daily habits and the news today?"</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What could I do better next time to participate more effectively in a group discussion about environmental topics?"</li>
        </ul></p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The "Panama's Wildlife News Discussion" worked well to engage students in authentic speaking, especially when discussing potential reasons for changes in animal behavior. Most students confidently used the present simple for habits, and 'there is/are' for descriptions. However, some struggled with forming accurate comparative adjectives spontaneously. Adjustments for next time: provide more structured sentence starters for comparatives during the practice phase and potentially a visual aid or chart for common comparative forms.</p>
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> The speaking activities and discussed news stories will serve as a foundation for Lesson 4 (Writing Composition). Students will use the ideas and vocabulary generated in this lesson to write a short news report or a descriptive paragraph about an animal's habits and any recent changes in its environment.</p>
      </td>
      <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">
        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___<br>5 min</p>
      </td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>